In _tau (dixon_coles.py:52-62), bound the correction term so it can't go negative regardless of how large lam_home/lam_away get, e.g.:

    if i == 0 and j == 1:
        return max(1 + lam_home * rho, 1e-6)
    if i == 1 and j == 0:
        return max(1 + lam_away * rho, 1e-6)

(or equivalently clamp lam_home/lam_away to at most 1/abs(rho) before applying the multiplier). This keeps the low-score correction non-negative for any reachable expected-goal rate while leaving the well-behaved (0,0)/(1,1) branches and all normal-scoring-league behavior untouched.

Wrap lines 201-202 in try/except Exception, log the error, and re-render predict.html with an error message (mirroring the /fixtures pattern) instead of adding a manual feature_names length check — sklearn Pipelines already validate input shape internally at predict time, and duplicating that check risks drifting out of sync with the pipeline itself. e.g.:
try:
    features = prepare_prediction_features(home_lookup, away_lookup, stats, build_h2h_map())
    result = model.predict(features)
except Exception as e:
    common["error"] = f"Could not generate a prediction (model/data mismatch): {e}"
    return render_template("predict.html", active="predict", prediction=None, **common)
Also consider setting debug=False (or reading from an env var) for any non-local deployment, since debug=True + an unhandled exception is a remote code execution console, not just an ugly 500.
